        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains December 12 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, such as 2 of 5.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For December 12 in music history, the Hot 100 chart week that includes December 12, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"You Light Up My Life" had the longest run, with ten weeks starting October 15, 1977, and December 12 fell in its ninth.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and one was in the final week of its run.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the December 12 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called December 12.</p>
        <p>This page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains December 12 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>

        <h2 id="early">December 12 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Motown Hit, a Funk Landmark and an Anthem</h2>
        <p>The first three Decembers of the decade brought a Motown classic, a drum-machine funk record and a song that became an anthem.</p>

        <h3>1970: "The Tears of a Clown" by Smokey Robinson and the Miracles</h3>
        <p>Stevie Wonder wrote the music, and Hank Cosby produced the instrumental track.</p>
        <p>Smokey Robinson wrote the lyrics and sang lead, and the song first appeared on the 1967 Miracles album Make It Happen.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Motown Britain released it as a single first, in July 1970, and it reached number one in the UK before the US version came out.</p>
        <p>Charles R. Sirard played the bassoon, which is one of the few hit pop singles to feature that instrument.</p>
        <p>It was the Miracles' first and only number one with Robinson as lead singer.</p>
        <p>The run began December 12, so the chart week that contains December 12 was the first of its two weeks.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Family Affair" by Sly and the Family Stone</h3>
        <p>Sly Stone wrote and produced "Family Affair," and it came from the album There's a Riot Goin' On.</p>
        <p>Epic released the single on November 6, 1971, and Wikipedia calls it the band's third and final number one pop single.</p>
        <p>It also topped the R&amp;B chart, and the drum-machine rhythm was reused later by the Temptations and by Stevie Wonder.</p>
        <p>The song is number 79 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>It spent three weeks at number one, and December 11 was the second.</p>

        <h3>1972: "I Am Woman" by Helen Reddy</h3>
        <p>Helen Reddy and Ray Burton, both Australian, wrote "I Am Woman."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it debuted at number 99 in the June 24, 1972 issue and reached number one in the December 9, 1972 issue.</p>
        <p>Reddy won the Grammy for Best Female Pop Vocal Performance, and it was the first Australian-penned song to win a Grammy.</p>
        <p>It was also the first Hot 100 number one by an Australian-born artist.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the week of December 9.</p>
        <p>More of her records are on <a href="/blog/artists/helen-reddy-songs/">Helen Reddy Songs</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">December 12 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Sibling Duo, a Kung Fu Craze and a German Disco Hit</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade brought a duo's second number one, a B-side that took over the charts and a German studio group.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Top of the World" by the Carpenters</h3>
        <p>Richard Carpenter wrote the music and John Bettis wrote the lyrics.</p>
        <p>The album version came from 1972, and the single version was recorded on August 15, 1973.</p>
        <p>Karen Carpenter re-recorded her lead vocal for the single because she was not satisfied with the original.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the single followed Lynn Anderson's country cover, which reached number two on the country chart.</p>
        <p>It was the duo's second number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>The song is number 39 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>It held the top for two weeks, and December 8 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>More of their records are on <a href="/blog/artists/carpenters-songs/">Carpenters Songs</a>.</p>

        <h3>1974: "Kung Fu Fighting" by Carl Douglas</h3>
        <p>Carl Douglas wrote "Kung Fu Fighting," and Biddu produced it.</p>
        <p>Biddu had hired Douglas to sing "I Want to Give You My Everything" and needed a B-side.</p>
        <p>Only about ten minutes of a three-hour session were left, so just two takes were recorded.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Robin Blanchflower of Pye Records insisted it be the A-side.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on both the Hot 100 and the Hot Soul Singles chart.</p>
        <p>The song is number 15 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Douglas also appears on the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-one-hit-wonders/">70s one-hit wonders</a> list.</p>
        <p>The run began December 7, so the week that contains December 12 was the first of two.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Fly, Robin, Fly" by Silver Convention</h3>
        <p>Sylvester Levay and Michael Kunze wrote "Fly, Robin, Fly," and it was the third single from the album Save Me.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it a chart-topper with only six words.</p>
        <p>Silver Convention became the second German act to top the US charts, after Bert Kaempfert.</p>
        <p>The song won the 1976 Grammy for Best R&amp;B Instrumental Performance.</p>
        <p>It held the top for three weeks, and December 6 was the second.</p>
        <p>The song is number 14 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="late">December 12 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: Two Long Runs, a Disco Comeback and a Power Ballad</h2>
        <p>The last four Decembers brought the decade's two longest runs, a disco record that kept returning and a decade-closing ballad.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" by Rod Stewart</h3>
        <p>Rod Stewart wrote "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)," and Tom Dowd produced it for the album A Night on the Town.</p>
        <p>Jerry Jumonville played the saxophone solo.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the BBC originally banned the song.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on November 13, 1976 and stayed there eight consecutive weeks, until January 1, 1977.</p>
        <p>December 11 was the fifth of those eight weeks.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1977: "You Light Up My Life" by Debby Boone</h3>
        <p>Joseph Brooks wrote "You Light Up My Life" and produced Debby Boone's version.</p>
        <p>It won him the Academy Award for Best Original Song and the Grammy for Song of the Year.</p>
        <p>Boone's version was recorded in April 1977 and released on August 16, 1977.</p>
        <p>It ran ten weeks at number one, from October 15 to December 17, 1977.</p>
        <p>December 10 was the ninth of the ten weeks.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Le Freak" by Chic</h3>
        <p>Bernard Edwards and Nile Rodgers of Chic wrote and produced "Le Freak."</p>
        <p>Atlantic released it on September 21, 1978, as the first single from C'est Chic.</p>
        <p>Rodgers says it was devised around New Year's Eve 1977 after he and Edwards were refused entry to Studio 54.</p>
        <p>It was Chic's first number one on the Hot 100, and it also topped the R&amp;B chart.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows six weeks at number one, in three separate stretches.</p>
        <p>The chart week of December 9 was its first, then Barbra Streisand and Neil Diamond took over, and it returned December 23 and again January 20.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Babe" by Styx</h3>
        <p>Dennis DeYoung wrote "Babe" as a birthday present for his wife, Suzanne.</p>
        <p>It was the lead single from the album Cornerstone, released on September 14, 1979.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was not meant for Styx at first, and J.Y. Young and Tommy Shaw convinced DeYoung to include his demo.</p>
        <p>It was Styx's first and only number one in the US, and the penultimate number one of the 1970s.</p>
        <p>It spent two weeks at number one, starting December 8.</p>

